  "slug": "archaea-duplication-d927",
  "summary": "New tools target consistent TE and gene-family recovery across genome panels, while curated databases extend comparative genomic resources.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is consistent recovery of repetitive and divergent sequence content across multiple genome assemblies instead of per-genome annotation. GeneFamHunter searches nucleotide assemblies with six-frame windows to retrieve divergent and tandemly arrayed gene-family members, reportedly outperforming annotation-based methods. Earl Grey ParTEA, a Snakemake pipeline, builds a shared non-redundant TE library across genome panels to make comparative TE annotation consistent from pangenomes to deep phylogenetic scopes. Both address annotation bias that confounds variation analysis in assemblies. AnnelidaDB (1121 annelid species, multi-omic) and DoriC 14.0 (prokaryotic replication origins, expanded plasmid oriV) are database resources that support comparative work but are not graph-based structural variation methods. The items do not demonstrate graph pangenome SV detection.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
